-The distance to entry/exit is derived from standard ray-AABB intersection, where $t_{min}$ and $t_{max}$ are computed for each axis and combined [S1, unofficial].
+**Ray-AABB Intersection Distances:**
+For each axis $i \in \{x, y, z\}$:
+$$ t_{1,i} = \frac{bbox_{min,i} - o_i}{d_i} $$
+$$ t_{2,i} = \frac{bbox_{max,i} - o_i}{d_i} $$
+$$ t_{enter,i} = \min(t_{1,i}, t_{2,i}) $$
+$$ t_{exit,i} = \max(t_{1,i}, t_{2,i}) $$
+$$ t_{min} = \max_i(t_{enter,i}) $$
+$$ t_{max} = \min_i(t_{exit,i}) $$
+Intersection exists if $t_{min} \le t_{max}$ and $t_{max} \ge 0$ [G].
 
